#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106763} :activity/id #uuid "eb9e7f19-fbe1-5bc0-8cac-285a26936c70" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368 :topic/id #uuid "99cc78ff-5612-5e6a-a347-8c2389044b69" :topic/math-academy-id 1290} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105651 :question/id #uuid "d044f208-be6c-508f-b92d-221f36a8edcf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7352"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357387}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "04131611-f675-5229-bd1f-811eaf724260" :step/math-academy-id 27298 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105652 :question/id #uuid "528a4e6c-6338-56ae-a56b-3de96b69b32c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7353"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53675175-8261-54ef-8dfb-2b3087b91239" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259722}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Probability Mass Functions"} :step/id #uuid "d66903ed-322e-5a89-82cf-d007695d5d4c" :step/math-academy-id 27299 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106762}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105653 :question/id #uuid "f70939b9-5f06-57e8-ac4a-d4217a12aa6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7354"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22f7889-e5e4-5237-bb3b-fe1572ef4fbf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410420}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "ec55bd0b-4d12-5185-b2f3-c2f5b6e10834" :step/math-academy-id 27300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105654 :question/id #uuid "c27e7286-1b80-532a-945e-e7897f4635eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-7355"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35193ec5-030d-5c78-b536-08e23bd3b7d4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049911}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351550}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within a Set of Values"} :step/id #uuid "c81f0294-20be-52d5-b339-bc0866cf7bdc" :step/math-academy-id 27301 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107103 :tutorial/content "A **random variable** is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nFor example, suppose we conduct an experiment where we flip a coin twice. The sample space for this experiment, which we'll denote as $\\Omega$ (the Greek capital letter \"Omega\") is given below:\n\n$$\n\\Omega = \\left\\{HH, HT, TH, TT\\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we define the random variable $X$ as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $X =$ \"*the number of heads we get when a coin is flipped twice*.\" \n\nLet's work out the possible values of $X.$\n\n- If we get two heads (i.e. the outcome of the experiment is an element of $\\{HH\\}$), then $X = 2.$\n\n- If we get one head (i.e. the outcome of the experiment is an element of $\\{HT,TH\\}$), then $X = 1.$\n\n- If we get no heads (i.e. the outcome of the experiment is an element of $\\{TT\\}$), then $X = 0.$\n\nSo, the possible values of $X$ are given by the following set:\n\n$$\nS = \\{0,1,2\\}\n$$\n\nInformally, if we flip a coin twice and count the number of heads, we can get either no heads, one head, or two heads. No other values are possible.\n\nThe set $S$ is called the **range** or **support** of $X,$ and it includes *every possible value* of the random variable $X.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0e1db88b-1f70-5049-bcce-35015bbe268c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4440 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d8be7abf-649f-5403-aa2f-6829e8676c16" :step/math-academy-id 27302 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106831}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107115 :tutorial/content "The **probability distribution**, or more precisely, **probability mass function** of a discrete random variable $X$ on a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ that maps each element $x \\in S$ to its probability of occurring:\n\n$$\nP(X = x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe above can be read as \"the probability that the random variable $X$ takes on the value $x,$ is equal to $f(x).$\"\n\nFor example, if $X$ is the result of rolling a $6$-sided die, then we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & x=1,2,3,4,5,6 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function tells us that\n\n- the probability of the die landing on any integer between $1$ and $6$ inclusive is $\\dfrac{1}{6},$ and\n\n- the probability of the die landing on any other number is zero.\n\nWe can also represent this probability mass function using a table, as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "66daf81e-f474-50f8-b670-e55e24eeb1db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4609 :tutorial/title "The Probability Mass Function for a Discrete Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "0cfde5a4-e03f-5b7b-b7a3-084b4e188e40" :step/math-academy-id 28351 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106832}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106831 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107132 :tutorial/content "A **discrete random variable** is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nNote that an interval of real numbers cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nFor example, the result of rolling a $6$-sided dice is a discrete random variable because its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\nOn the other hand, the temperature outside is *not* a discrete random variable because its possible values form an interval of real numbers. The temperature could be anywhere between, say, $50^\\circ \\, \\textrm F$ and $90^\\circ \\, \\textrm F,$ and the interval of real numbers $[50, 90]$ cannot be listed in a sequence." :tutorial/id #uuid "41fdd07b-af71-5dd7-8541-e3c161774315" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6286 :tutorial/title "Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "ee2b1f28-9218-55e7-85d6-9d0eb6abb586" :step/math-academy-id 38089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106759}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106832 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107133 :tutorial/content "In general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function for a random variable $X$ on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to the conditions above.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$ states that any possible outcome for $X$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1.$\n\n- The condition $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ must add up to $1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e714d0c9-9e63-52c5-a365-16afc370e98e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6287 :tutorial/title "Conditions for a Function To Be a Valid Probability Mass Function"} :step/id #uuid "3e5fcbe4-37c4-5421-b3a8-e040c80da52d" :step/math-academy-id 38090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106760}}] :activity/title "Probability Mass Functions of Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105966}]}
